[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This right here is my Samsung S23, but there is something a little unusual about it. When I unlock it, it opens straight into Linux. It is running Linux with Fosh, an interface made for phones. It has an app drawer, notifications, quick settings, and multitasking. From'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here, I can install Linux apps, use a terminal, run coding tools, and even play a Linux game at 60 FPS. But Android is still here. I can open the normal Android camera, gallery, and other'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Android apps from the same app drawer. So, I did not replace Android with Linux. I made both of them work together. Even after a reboot, it takes me straight into Linux. I never see the normal Android home screen. This makes'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the phone feel Linux first. &gt;&gt; If you want to install Linux on your phone, there are already some great options like Ubuntu Touch, Mobian, and PostmarketOS, but the problem is device support. They only work on few devices.'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I wanted to build something different. Instead of removing Android at all, Android is built on top of Linux kernel. So, why not use that kernel to run Linux on Android?'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Now, let\'s see what it can actually run. FlatHub is already set up here. It lets me install Linux apps almost like I would install apps from the Play Store. To test it, I installed LocalSend. It installed normally, appeared in the app'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drawer, and worked without any problems. Then, I tried something more useful. I installed VS Code and opened a small Python project. VS Code was running locally on the phone. I ran the Python project, and it worked. Of course, there'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is also a proper Linux terminal. I am using an Ubuntu root file system inside a CH root, so I can use APT and install compatible Debian packages. I can also install .deb files. The phone uses an'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ARM processor, so an application needs to support ARM Linux. But not every Linux application will work. For example, I also tried installing Steam. It installed, but it never launched. Wayland apps work best.'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'X86-only apps may not work or may run slowly, but there are still many Linux apps and tools that I can install here. Before you think this setup must be slow, I wanted to test the GPU. For that, I installed SuperTuxKart. This is'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a real 3D Linux game, and on my Samsung S23, it was running at around 60 FPS. The game was smooth, and I did not notice any major lag. So, hardware acceleration is working inside the Linux'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment. This is also why Fosh and the other Linux apps feel smooth. Now comes to my favorite part of setup. I still have Android when I need it. For example, Android already has very good'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'support for the cameras on this phone. So, I do not need to fight with Linux camera drivers. I can open the normal Android camera directly from the Fosh app drawer. I can take a photo and then open the normal Android gallery from the same app drawer. These are real Android'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'applications. They simply launch from Fosh. There is also a shared folder between Android and Linux. So, I can take a photo in Android and open the same file inside Linux. I can also create a file in Linux and access it'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from Android. This is why I decided to keep both systems. Android handles the things it is already good at. Linux gives me the apps, terminal, and tools I want. This project is completely open source,'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and if you want to support my work, subscribing to the channel really helps. So far, I have tested this setup on four phones. They are the Samsung S23, Pixel 6, Moto G71, and Poco F1. There is one important requirement. Your'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'phone needs to be rooted. This is because the Linux system is running through CHroot. To make the process easier, I created a native Android application for it. You install the app and give it root access. The app'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'configures the Ubuntu file system and tools automatically, so you do not need to enter a huge list of commands manually. I have uploaded the app and the complete project to GitHub. You can find the link in the description.'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The application also has extra settings. For example, you can enable immersive mode to give Fosh more screen space. There are also options to disable Android apps and services you do not'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need. For example, you can disable Google Play services and other apps running in the background. This can free up some RAM and reduce background processing. You can also set native OS as the default Android launcher.'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is why it opens automatically after every reboot. I do not need to open the Linux app manually. I just unlock the phone and it takes me straight into Fosh. On my Samsung S23, I went one step'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'further. I first installed Lineage OS without Google apps. Then I installed this Linux setup on top of it. So Android is still there for hardware support, but most of the Google software is gone.'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, if you want to try this project, but maybe your phone is not rooted, or maybe you do not want to root your main phone. For that, I have another project called Droid Desk. It also runs Linux on Android, but it uses a different method.'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It does not give you exactly the same experience as this setup, but it is an easier option for people who do not want root access. I will link the Droid Desk app and the full Droid Desk video in the description.'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For now, I think this hybrid version makes the most sense. Android handles the phone hardware, Linux gives me the applications and tools I want, and Fosh brings both of them together in one interface. It is not perfect yet, but it works. And I think this is only'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the beginning.'
